  "query_preview": "..A ridge of high pressure will shift across Arizona  \ninto Tuesday. This will result in hot temperatures, with highs \npeaking Monday when the ridge axis is overhead. High temperatures \nwill top out 10-15 degrees above normal. Most lower elevation \nlocations from Tucson westward and in the Gila River valley will be \nin Moderate HeatRisk through Tuesday, with localized Major HeatRisk \nin the far western deserts around Ajo and Organ Pipe on Monday. \nHighs Monday will be 101-103 degrees in the Tucson metro and 105-108 \ndegrees across central/western Pima County. \n\nSurface high pressure will build down the front range of the Rockies \ninto west Texas late tonight into Monday, resulting in gusty east to \nsoutheast winds across portions of southeast Arizona Monday. Wind \nspeeds peak Monday morning, with gusts of 25-35 mph from Tucson \neastward in areas not sheltered from easterly winds. Wind speeds \nthen taper off rapidly Monday afternoon. \n\nAs the ridge axis slides off to our east on Tuesday and Wednesday, \nthe steering flow turns south/southeasterly which will advect some \nmid-level moisture into our eastern areas (mainly east of Tucson). \nMeasurable rainfall chances remain very low. However, there may be \nenough moisture for a few lightning strikes/dry thunderstorms and \nsome gusty outflow winds. Thunder probabilities Tuesday/Wednesday \nare in the 5-15 percent range east of Tucson, with the highest \nchances over the higher terrain and along the New Mexico state line. \nHigh temperatures lower 4-6 degrees by Wednesday, but will still be \n6-8 degrees above normal.   \n\nThe mid level moisture gets pushed to our east late Wednesday night \nand Thursday, as a storm system tracks to our north across the Great \nBasin and the flow turns southwesterly. As this system moves into \nthe High Plains Friday, our flow then turns westerly. Lowering \nheights Thursday and Friday will shave another couple of degrees off \nof high temperatures, with highs lowering to 4-6 degrees above \nnormal. Ridging then begins to build back across the area next \nweekend for a return to warmer high temperatures.",
